# PURGATORY core

PURGATORY is a custom-built native 2D side-scrolling MMORPG. This repository is the project root: a Cargo virtual workspace for a dedicated headless server, a native desktop client, shared crates, and development tools.

## Accepted stack

- Stable Rust, 2024 edition, managed with `rustup` and Cargo. Nightly is not used. Language or toolchain changes need an explicit owner decision (ADR-0001).
- Custom engine. Unreal, Unity, Godot, Bevy, and other game engines are forbidden unless the owner changes ADR-0002. `winit` and `wgpu` are client platform/graphics libraries, not a game engine. egui is client-only development overlay tooling, not production game UI (ADR-0016).
- Server-authoritative simulation (ADR-0003). Clients send intents and inputs. Clients never send authoritative results such as position, damage, loot grants, XP, or collision outcomes.
- Workspace lint: `unsafe_code = deny`.

## Crate roles

| Path | Crate | Role |
|---|---|---|
| `apps/client` | `purgatory-client` | Native desktop client |
| `apps/server` | `purgatory-server` | Headless dedicated server |
| `apps/dev_hub` | `purgatory-dev-hub` | Developer Hub GUI (provisional eframe; ADR-0052) |
| `crates/common` | `purgatory-common` | Shared primitives |
| `crates/simulation` | `purgatory-simulation` | Authoritative simulation |
| `crates/protocol` | `purgatory-protocol` | Client/server protocol types and version semantics |
| `crates/content` | `purgatory-content` | Content definition loading and validation boundary |
| `crates/persistence` | `purgatory-persistence` | File-backed character identity and persistent character state |
| `crates/skeleton` | `purgatory-skeleton` | Presentation skeleton math (Definition + local Pose → world Pose). No GPU, simulation, or network. |
| `crates/animation` | `purgatory-animation` | Presentation clip sampling (AnimationClip + sample → LocalPose). Depends only on `purgatory-skeleton`. |
| `crates/dev_runtime` | `purgatory-dev-runtime` | Headless Developer Hub orchestration (no GUI) |
| `tools/content_validator` | `purgatory-content-validator` | Content validation tool |
| `tools/animation_lab` | `purgatory-animation-lab` | Standalone A7.0 Animation Lab (eframe; Hub-launched; ADR-0058) |
| `tools/bot_client` | `purgatory-bot-client` / `purgatory-load` | Headless QUIC load harness |

Do not nest another `PURGATORY/` directory. Do not treat a parent folder as the project repo.

## Source of truth

Repository code and documentation are the durable source of truth. Do not invent project facts.

Consult, do not duplicate volatile numbers from:

- `PURGATORY_CURSOR_MASTER_EXECUTION_PLAN.md` — master execution specification
- `README.md` — workspace map, current status, how to run
- `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` — structural rules and ownership
- `docs/DECISIONS.md` — Architecture Decision Records (ADRs)
- `docs/PROTOCOL.md` — wire protocol and trust boundary
- `docs/ROADMAP.md` — phase order and status
- `docs/TEST_GATES.md` — recorded verification gates
- `docs/PERFORMANCE_BUDGETS.md` — living performance budgets
- `docs/CONTENT_PIPELINE.md` — content authoring boundary
- `docs/DIAGNOSTICS_ARCHITECTURE.md` — client debug/diagnostics split (D-track)
- root `PHASE` — current phase marker

`.cursor/rules/` constrain agent behavior. They do not replace the documents above.

## Change policy

- Understand existing architecture before modifying it.
- Extend existing systems. Do not replace or duplicate them by default.
- Major technology, engine, protocol, or architecture changes require explicit approval (normally a new or updated ADR).
- Keep current caps, delays, timeouts, protocol version, tick-rate constants, load-test parameters, and other phase-specific numbers in code and project docs. Do not copy them into these rules.
